<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · Dance · 1861 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Korobeiniki (The Peddlers)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ru">Коробейники</bdi> / <bdi lang="ru-Latn">Korobeiniki</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Russian folk song from an 1861 poem about a peddler meeting a girl. Its quick tune became famous as the music of the game Tetris.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Nikolay Nekrasov</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (early arrangements published by Yakov Prigozhy and Alexander N. Chernyavsky, 1898)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Russian folk song</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Russia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem printed 1861; first musical settings published 1898</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song · Dance</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics from the poem by Nikolay Nekrasov (printed 1861))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Korobeiniki&quot; (&quot;The Peddlers&quot;) is a nineteenth-century Russian folk song. A korobeinik was a travelling peddler, and the song tells of one such peddler meeting a girl, their bargaining over his goods standing for courtship. Its words come from a poem of the same name by Nikolay Nekrasov, first printed in the magazine Sovremennik in 1861.</p><p>The song's quickening tempo and its dance character soon made it a popular folk song. The lyrics exist in several variants, all small variations on the beginning of Nekrasov's poem. Early musical arrangements were published by Yakov Prigozhy and Alexander N. Chernyavsky in 1898, and Varvara Panina recorded it for several gramophone companies from 1905. In 1950 Kostya Poliansky and his Balalaika Orchestra released an instrumental recording, and later performers in Russian included Ivan Rebroff (1968), the Alexandrov Ensemble (1988) and Chanticleer (1997).</p><p>Outside Russia the tune is best known as the theme of Tetris. Arrangements of it first appeared in Spectrum Holobyte's Apple IIGS and Mac versions of the game; in 1989 Hirokazu Tanaka arranged it again as the &quot;Type A&quot; music of Nintendo's Game Boy version. That arrangement differs a little from the folk melody and became closely tied to the game in Western popular culture. Since 2002 the Tetris Company has required it in every version of the game, and it holds a sound trademark on this variation for use in video games. In 2008 UGO ranked it the third-best piece of video game music of all time.</p><p>The melody has been recorded in many other styles. A Eurodance cover named &quot;Tetris&quot; by Doctor Spin reached No. 6 on the UK singles chart in 1992; other versions range from ska and string-quartet recordings to &quot;Hold on Tight&quot; (2023), performed by aespa and featured in the film Tetris, which samples the game's theme. The Timbers Army sings the melody with altered words at Portland Timbers games, usually with a simple dance.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A light, fast song of a meeting on the road: a peddler shows his wares to a girl, and their haggling over the price becomes a playful courtship. The bargaining is the song's way of speaking about attraction.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Russian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Russian</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">«Ой, полна, полна коробушка,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">«Oj, polna, polna korobuška,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“Oh, full, full is my little box,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Есть и ситцы и парча.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Estʹ i sitcy i parča.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There are calicoes and brocade.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Пожалей, моя зазнобушка,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Požalej, moja zaznobuška,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Take pity, my sweetheart,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Молодецкого плеча!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Molodeckogo pleča!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On a young fellow's shoulder!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Выди, выди в рожь высокую!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Vydi, vydi v rožʹ vysokuju!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come out, come out into the tall rye!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Там до ночки погожу,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Tam do nočki pogožu,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There I will wait until nightfall,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">А завижу черноокую —</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">A zavižu černookuju —</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And when I catch sight of the dark-eyed one —</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Все товары разложу.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Vse tovary razložu.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I will lay out all my wares.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Цены сам платил не малые.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Ceny sam platil ne malye.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I paid no small prices myself.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Не торгуйся, не скупись:</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Ne torgujsja, ne skupisʹ:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do not haggle, do not be stingy:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Подставляй-ка губы алые,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Podstavljaj-ka guby alye,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, hold up your scarlet lips,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Ближе к милому садись!»</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Bliže k milomu sadisʹ!»</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Sit closer to your dear one!”</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Вот и пала ночь туманная,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Vot i pala nočʹ tumannaja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And now the misty night has fallen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Ждет удалый молодец.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Žaet udalyj molodec.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The bold young fellow waits.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Чу, идет! — пришла желанная,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Ču, idet! — prišla želannaja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hark, she comes! — the longed-for one has come,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Продает товар купец.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Prodaet tovar kupec.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The merchant sells his wares.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Катя бережно торгуется,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Katja berežno torguetsja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Katya bargains carefully,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Всё боится передать.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Vsë boitsja peredatʹ.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She keeps fearing to pay too much.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Парень с девицей цалуется,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Parenʹ s devicej caluetsja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The lad and the girl kiss,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Просит цену набавлять.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Prosit cenu nabavljatʹ.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He asks her to raise the price.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Знает только ночь глубокая,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Znaet tolʹko nočʹ glubokaja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Only the deep night knows</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Как поладили они.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Kak poladili oni.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>How they came to terms.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Распрямись ты, рожь высокая,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Rasprjamisʹ ty, rožʹ vysokaja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Straighten up, you tall rye,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Тайну свято сохрани!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Tajnu svjato soxrani!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Keep the secret sacredly!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Nikolay Nekrasov, Korobeiniki (1861), the opening. Text from <a href="https://ru.wikisource.org/wiki/%D0%9A%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%B5%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B8_(%D0%9D%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B2)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (scientific transliteration).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>IMPORT: orig lines 1.8 and 2.6 begin with Wikisource's verse numbers ('20 Все товары разложу.', '30 Всё боится передать.'): strip them from orig; en and rom leave them out.</li><li>line 1.1: 'коробушка' is a diminutive of 'короб', the pedlar's box or pack carried on the back (the korobeiniki are the pedlars who carry it).</li><li>line 1.3-1.4: 'take pity on a young fellow's shoulder' means: buy something and lighten the load he carries.</li><li>line 1.5: 'Выди' is a folk/poetic form of 'выйди' (come out).</li><li>line 2.6: 'передать' here means to overpay, to give more than the thing is worth.</li><li>line 2.7: 'цалуется' is the older spelling of 'целуется' (kisses); romanized as written.</li><li>line 2.8: 'asks her to raise the price' plays on the bargaining: he asks for more in return (more kisses). The subject is the lad.</li><li>line 1.3: 'зазнобушка', an affectionate folk word for a sweetheart (from 'зазноба').</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
